[0.117, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?7l\r\u001b[13A\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mRecommended\u001b[m: Implement the \u001b[1mdeterministic Electrical Value Parser & Normalizer\u001b[m for resistance, capacitance,\u001b[?7h\u001b[112G\r\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n⣽  \u001b[94mG\u001b[m\n\n\n\b\b\u001b[?2026l"]
[0.100, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[29AHow should the application parse, normalize, and display these values when you enter a component, import a BOM, or\r\n  type into the search bar?\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  If we build a deterministic \u001b[1mElectrical Value Parser & Normalizer\u001b[m in Rust:\r\n\u001b[K\n  1. \u001b[1mOn input (Component creation or BOM entry)\u001b[m: Typing any notation (e.g., \u001b[94m4k7\u001b[m or \u001b[94m0.047uF\u001b[m or \u001b[94m100kA\u001b[m) automatically\r\n  parses and stores:\u001b[K\r\n      • The \u001b[1mcanonical numeric base value\u001b[m for exact comparison & sorting (\u001b[94mresistance_ohms = 4700.0\u001b[m as \u001b[94mf64\u001b[m,\n\u001b[99D\u001b[94mcapacitance_pf = 47000.0\u001b[m as \u001b[94mf64\u001b[m, and for pots, extracts \u001b[94mtaper = \"A\"\u001b[m + \u001b[94mresistance_ohms = 10\u001b[4b.0\u001b[m).\u001b[K\n\u001b[98D• A \u001b[1mcanonical display string\u001b[m formatted in standard engineering notation (e.g., \u001b[94m4.7kΩ\u001b[m, \u001b[94m47nF\u001b[m — with an option to\r\n\u001b[6Cprefer \u001b[94mnF\u001b[m for the \u001b[94m1nF–999nF\u001b[m audio range rather than \u001b[94m0.001µF–0.99µF\u001b[m).\u001b[K\r\n  2. \u001b[1mOn search\u001b[m: Typing \u001b[94m4k7\u001b[m, \u001b[94m4.7k\u001b[m, or \u001b[94m4700\u001b[m into"]
[0.000, "o", " the search filter parses the query token into the same canonical\r\n  numeric value (\u001b[94m4700.0 Ω\u001b[m) so it matches the component regardless of how it was originally entered, while also\u001b[K\r\n  falling back to substring/text matching for part numbers (\u001b[94mTL072\u001b[m, \u001b[94m1N4148\u001b[m, \u001b[94mDaisy\u001b[m).\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\u001b[?7l\n\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mRecommended\u001b[m: Implement the \u001b[1mdeterministic Electrical Value Parser & Normalizer\u001b[m for resistance, capacitance,\u001b[?7h\u001b[112G\u001b[?7l\r\n\u001b[K  inductance, voltage, and potentiometer taper-value codes (\u001b[94mA100k\u001b[m/\u001b[94m100kA\u001b[m), storing canonical numeric columns\u001b[?7h\u001b[108G\r\n  (\u001b[94mresistance_ohms\u001b[m, \u001b[94mcapacitance_pf\u001b[m, \u001b[94minductance_uh\u001b[m, \u001b[94mvoltage_rating_volts\u001b[m) and formatting capacitance in the \u001b[94m1nF–999nF\u001b[m\r\n  range as \u001b[94mnF\u001b[m by default. Does this match how you want value entry and search to behave?\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n\u001b[90m───────────────────────────────────────────────────"]
[0.000, "o", "─────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m> I'd like to store the original spec string alongside a canonicalized value. A lot of the values include additional\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m  information, like variance and pot curve that are important\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  That's a crucial distinction—preserving the \u001b[1mraw spec string\u001b[m (e.g., \u001b[94m100kA 16mm\u001b[m, \u001b[94m4k7 1% 1/4W\u001b[m, \u001b[94m0.047uF 63V Box\u001b[m, \u001b[94mB50K\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94mCenter Detent\u001b[m) verb\u001b[K\r\n⣾  \u001b[94mGen\u001b[m\n\n\n\u001b[4D\u001b[?2026l"]
[0.016, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?7l\r\u001b[14A\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mRecommended\u001b[m: Implement the \u001b[1mdeterministic Electrical Value Parser & Normalizer\u001b[m for resistance, capacitance,\u001b[?7h\u001b[112G\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u001b[90Datim alongside the extra\n\n\n\n\u001b[43D\u001b[?2026l"]
[0.017, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?7l\r\u001b[14A\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mRecommended\u001b[m: Implement the \u001b[1mdeterministic Electrical Value Parser & Normalizer\u001b[m for resistance, capacitance,\u001b[?7h\u001b[112G\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u001b[66Dcted/canonic\n\n\n\n\u001b[55D\u001b[?2026l"]
