[0.084, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\r\u001b[29A  ➡️ \u001b[1mRecommended\u001b[m: Implement the \u001b[1mdeterministic Electrical Value Parser & Normalizer\u001b[m for resistance, capacitance,\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[?5W\u001b[J  inductance, voltage, and potentiometer taper-value codes (\u001b[94mA100k\u001b[m/\u001b[94m100kA\u001b[m), storing canonical numeric columns\r\n  (\u001b[94mresistance_ohms\u001b[m, \u001b[94mcapacitance_pf\u001b[m, \u001b[94minductance_uh\u001b[m, \u001b[94mvoltage_rating_volts\u001b[m) and formatting capacitance in the \u001b[94m1nF–999nF\u001b[m\r\n  range as \u001b[94mnF\u001b[m by default. Does this match how you want value entry and search to behave?\r\n\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m> I'd like to store the original spec string alongside a canonicalized value. A lot of the values include additional\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m  information, like variance and pot curve that are important\u001b[m\r\n\n  That's a crucial distinction—preserving the \u001b[1mraw spec string\u001b[m (e.g., \u001b[94m100kA 16m"]
[0.000, "o", "m\u001b[m, \u001b[94m4k7 1% 1/4W\u001b[m, \u001b[94m0.047uF 63V Box\u001b[m, \u001b[94mB50K\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94mCenter Detent\u001b[m) verbatim alongside the extracted/canonicalized numeric and parametric fields ensures nothing entered\r\n  or imported from a schematic/BOM is ever lost, while still enabling normalized numeric search, sorting, and BOM\r\n  matching.\r\n  ──────\u001b[?7l\r\n\u001b[K  ❓ \u001b[1mQ4\u001b[m - \u001b[1mPhysical Storage Locations\u001b[m: How do you organize parts on your bench/workshop (e.g., Akro-Mils drawer\u001b[?7h\u001b[111G\r\n  cabinets, resistor/capacitor component binders with pages/sleeves, SMD books, tackle boxes, shelf bins), and should\r\n  a \u001b[1mStorage Location\u001b[m be a structured hierarchical entity (e.g., \u001b[94mContainer > Drawer/Page/Slot\u001b[m like \u001b[94mCabinet A > Row 3\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94mCol 2\u001b[m or \u001b[94mResistor Binder > Page 4\u001b[m) or a flat label/code? Also, can a single \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m live in only one storage\r\n  location, or split across multiple locations (e.g., working bench drawer + bulk overflow bin)?\r\n\n  Let's compare the two main models:\r\n\n "]
[0.000, "o", " 1. \u001b[1mSingle Primary Location per Component + Two-Level Hierarchy (\u001b[94;22mStorage Container\u001b[39;1m → \u001b[94;22mSlot / Label\u001b[39;1m)\u001b[m:\n\u001b[94D• You define \u001b[1mStorage Containers\u001b[m (e.g., \u001b[94mBinder 1: 1/4W Metal Film\u001b[m, \u001b[94mCabinet A: IC & Transistors\u001b[m, \u001b[94mBox 3: Pots &\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[94mJacks\u001b[m, \u001b[94mShelf: Enclosures & Modules\u001b[m) and each \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m has a \u001b[94mcontainer_id\u001b[m + a freeform \u001b[94mslot_label\u001b[m (e.g., \u001b[94mPage 3,\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[94mRow 2\u001b[m, \u001b[94mDrawer B4\u001b[m, \u001b[94mBag 12\u001b[m) pl\r\n\n⡿  \u001b[94mWo\u001b[90mrking...\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[90m└ Tip: To disable tips, toggle Show Tips in /settings.\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[94m>\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[90m──────────"]
