[1.299, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\r\n\n\u001b[J\u001b[31A  or imported from a schematic/BOM is ever lost, while still enabling normalized numeric search, sorting, and BOM\r\n  matching.\u001b[?7l\r\n\u001b[K  ──────\u001b[?7h\u001b[9G\u001b[?7l\r\n\u001b[K  ❓ \u001b[1mQ4\u001b[m - \u001b[1mPhysical Storage Locations\u001b[m: How do you organize parts on your bench/workshop (e.g., Akro-Mils drawer\u001b[?7h\u001b[111G\r\n  cabinets, resistor/capacitor component binders with pages/sleeves, SMD books, tackle boxes, shelf bins), and should\r\n  a \u001b[1mStorage Location\u001b[m be a structured hierarchical entity (e.g., \u001b[94mContainer > Drawer/Page/Slot\u001b[m like \u001b[94mCabinet A > Row 3\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94mCol 2\u001b[m or \u001b[94mResistor Binder > Page 4\u001b[m) or a flat label/code? Also, can a single \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m live in only one storage\r\n  location, or split across multiple locations (e.g., working bench drawer + bulk overflow bin)?\r\n\u001b[K\n  Let's compare the two main models:\r\n\u001b[K\n  1. \u001b[1mSingle Primary Location per Component + Two-Level Hierarchy (\u001b[94;22mStorage Container\u001b[39;1m → \u001b[94;22mSlot / Label\u001b[39;1m)\u001b[m:\u001b[K\n\u001b[94D• "]
[0.000, "o", "You define \u001b[1mStorage Containers\u001b[m (e.g., \u001b[94mBinder 1: 1/4W Metal Film\u001b[m, \u001b[94mCabinet A: IC & Transistors\u001b[m, \u001b[94mBox 3: Pots &\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[94mJacks\u001b[m, \u001b[94mShelf: Enclosures & Modules\u001b[m) and each \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m has a \u001b[94mcontainer_id\u001b[m + a freeform \u001b[94mslot_label\u001b[m (e.g., \u001b[94mPage 3,\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[94mRow 2\u001b[m, \u001b[94mDrawer B4\u001b[m, \u001b[94mBag 12\u001b[m) plus an optional \u001b[94moverflow_location\u001b[m note.\u001b[K\n\u001b[66D• Keeping a single \u001b[94mquantity_on_hand\u001b[m and primary location (\u001b[94mContainer\u001b[m + \u001b[94mSlot\u001b[m) per Component makes inline \u001b[94m+1 / -1\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6Cstock adjustments and BOM part-pulling instant without asking \u001b[3m\"which bin are you taking this 10k resistor\u001b[m\r\n      \u001b[3mfrom?\"\u001b[m every time you build a kit.\u001b[K\r\n  2. \u001b[1mMulti-Location Stock Balances (Many-to-Many \u001b[94;22mComponent\u001b[39;1m ↔ \u001b[94;22mStorage Location\u001b[39;1m with separate quantities per bin)\u001b[m:\r\n\u001b[6C• Every stock adjustment or BOM consumption requires choosing which location's balance to decrement.\u001b[?7l\r\n\n\u001b[K\u001b[?7h\u001b[1G\u001b[?7l\n\u001b[K  "]
[0.000, "o", "➡️ \u001b[1mRecommended\u001b[m: Model \u001b[1mStorage Location\u001b[m as a \u001b[1mStorage Container\u001b[m entity (with name, type like \u001b[94mDrawer Cabinet\u001b[m / \u001b[94mBinder\u001b[?7h\u001b[117G\u001b[m\r\n  / \u001b[94mBox\u001b[m / \u001b[94mShelf\u001b[m, and description) paired with a \u001b[94mslot_label\u001b[m on the Component (plus an optional\u001b[K\r\n  \u001b[94msecondary_location_notes\u001b[m field), keeping a single \u001b[94mquantity_on_hand\u001b[m per Component so stock adjustments and kit\r\n  kitting stay one-click fast. Does this match how your physical storage works?\r\n\u001b[K\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[94m>\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[90m───────────────────────────────────────────"]
[0.000, "o", "─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[96C\u001b[1K \u001b[2mGemini 4 Argon · medium\r\u001b[m\u001b[2A\u001b[2C\u001b[?2026l"]
[0.019, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\r\u001b[28A  or imported from a schematic/BOM is ever lost, while still enabling normalized numeric search, sorting, and BOM\u001b[K\r\n  matching.\u001b[K\r\n  ──────\u001b[K\r\n  ❓ \u001b[1mQ4\u001b[m - \u001b[1mPhysical Storage Locations\u001b[m: How do you organize parts on your bench/workshop (e.g., Akro-Mils drawer\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[?5W\u001b[J  cabinets, resistor/capacitor component binders with pages/sleeves, SMD books, tackle boxes, shelf bins), and should\r\n  a \u001b[1mStorage Location\u001b[m be a structured hierarchical entity (e.g., \u001b[94mContainer > Drawer/Page/Slot\u001b[m like \u001b[94mCabinet A > Row 3\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94mCol 2\u001b[m or \u001b[94mResistor Binder > Page 4\u001b[m) or a flat label/code? Also, can a single \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m live in only one storage\r\n  location, or split across multiple locations (e.g., working bench drawer + bulk overflow bin)?\r\n\n  Let's compare the two main models:\r\n\n  1. \u001b[1mSingle Primary Location per Component + Two-Level Hierarchy (\u001b[94;22mStorage Container\u001b[39;1m → \u001b[94;22mSlot / Label\u001b[39;1m)\u001b[m:\n\u001b[94D• You define \u001b[1mStorage Contain"]
[0.000, "o", "ers\u001b[m (e.g., \u001b[94mBinder 1: 1/4W Metal Film\u001b[m, \u001b[94mCabinet A: IC & Transistors\u001b[m, \u001b[94mBox 3: Pots &\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[94mJacks\u001b[m, \u001b[94mShelf: Enclosures & Modules\u001b[m) and each \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m has a \u001b[94mcontainer_id\u001b[m + a freeform \u001b[94mslot_label\u001b[m (e.g., \u001b[94mPage 3,\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[94mRow 2\u001b[m, \u001b[94mDrawer B4\u001b[m, \u001b[94mBag 12\u001b[m) plus an optional \u001b[94moverflow_location\u001b[m note.\n\u001b[66D• Keeping a single \u001b[94mquantity_on_hand\u001b[m and primary location (\u001b[94mContainer\u001b[m + \u001b[94mSlot\u001b[m) per Component makes inline \u001b[94m+1 / -1\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6Cstock adjustments and BOM part-pulling instant without asking \u001b[3m\"which bin are you taking this 10k resistor\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[3mfrom?\"\u001b[m every time you build a kit.\r\n  2. \u001b[1mMulti-Location Stock Balances (Many-to-Many \u001b[94;22mComponent\u001b[39;1m ↔ \u001b[94;22mStorage Location\u001b[39;1m with separate quantities per bin)\u001b[m:\r\n\u001b[6C• Every stock adjustment or BOM consumption requires choosing which location's balance to decrement.\u001b[?7l\r\n\n\n\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mRecommended\u001b[m: Model \u001b[1mStorage Location\u001b[m a"]
[0.000, "o", "s a \u001b[1mStorage Container\u001b[m entity (with name, type like \u001b[94mDrawer Cabinet\u001b[m / \u001b[94mBinder\u001b[?7h\u001b[117G\u001b[m\r\n  / \u001b[94mBox\u001b[m / \u001b[94mShelf\u001b[m, and description) paired with a \u001b[94mslot_label\u001b[m on the Component (plus an optional\r\n  \u001b[94msecondary_location_notes\u001b[m field), keeping a single \u001b[94mquantity_on_hand\u001b[m per Component so stock adjustments and kit\r\n  kitting stay one-click fast. Does this match how your physical storage works?\r\n\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m> I currently have a mixture of boxes with parts like partially assembled kits, crates with bags of components, and in\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m  some cases I have crates with bags full of bagged components. Given I need to move the bags of components around it\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m  is probably worth having a heirarichal concept for storage\u001b[m\r\n⣯  \u001b[94mWorki\u001b[90mng...\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[90m─────────"]
