[0.016, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\r\n\n\n\u001b[?5W\u001b[31A  That makes total sense: if you have a \u001b[1mCrate\u001b[m containing large \u001b[1mBags\u001b[m or \u001b[1mBoxes\u001b[m, which in turn contain smaller \u001b[1mBags\u001b[m of\r\n  components (or partially assembled kits), and you move a whole bag from one crate/box to another, a true \u001b[1mself-\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n  \u001b[1mreferential hierarchy (\u001b[94;22mparent_location_id\u001b[39;1m)\u001b[m means moving one bag updates the location path of every component and\r\n  sub-bag inside it in a single operation.\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  Let's sharpen that into a domain concept (\u001b[1mStorage Location\u001b[m) and check how \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m quantities interact with it:\r\n\u001b[K\u001b[?7l\n\u001b[K  ❓ \u001b[1mQ5\u001b[m - \u001b[1mHierarchical Storage Locations & Moving Bags\u001b[m: If we model \u001b[1mStorage Location\u001b[m as a self-referential tree (\u001b[94mid\u001b[m,\u001b[?7h\u001b[117G\u001b[?7l\r\n\u001b[K  \u001b[94mname\u001b[m, \u001b[94mkind\u001b[m such as \u001b[94mCrate\u001b[m / \u001b[94mBox\u001b[m / \u001b[94mBinder\u001b[m / \u001b[94mBag\u001b[m / \u001b[94mDrawer\u001b[m, \u001b[94mparent_id\u001b[m, \u001b[94mnotes\u001b[m)—for example, \u001b[94mCrate 1 > Audio IC Bag > Bag\u001b[?7h\u001b[118G\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94m#4\u001b[m—so you c"]
[0.000, "o", "an reparent any \u001b[94mStorage Location\u001b[m (and everything nested inside it moves automatically):\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  Can the \u001b[1msame Component\u001b[m exist in \u001b[1mmultiple Storage Locations at once\u001b[m (e.g., 20x \u001b[94mTL072\u001b[m in \u001b[94mCrate 1 > Bulk Bag\u001b[m AND 5x\r\n  \u001b[94mTL072\u001b[m in \u001b[94mPedal Box B > Active Parts Bag\u001b[m), OR does each \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m record point to \u001b[1mone\u001b[m \u001b[94mStorage Location\u001b[m (the specific\r\n  inner bag/drawer where that stash lives)?\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  • \u001b[1mOption A — One \u001b[94;22mStorage Location\u001b[39;1m per \u001b[94;22mComponent\u001b[39;1m record\u001b[m: Every \u001b[94mComponent\u001b[m points to a single leaf (or intermediate)\r\n  \u001b[94mStorage Location\u001b[m (\u001b[94mlocation_id\u001b[m). If you move that bag to another crate/box, the \u001b[94mStorage Location\u001b[m's \u001b[94mparent_id\u001b[m changes\r\n  and the breadcrumb (\u001b[94mCrate 2 > Bag A\u001b[m) updates everywhere. If you ever keep a separate stash in a Kit Build's box,\u001b[K\r\n  that's handled by allocating parts to the \u001b[1mKit Build\u001b[m (which can also have its own \u001b[94mStorage Location\u001b[m, e.g.,"]
[0.000, "o", " \u001b[94mBox: Klon\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94mClone WIP\u001b[m).\u001b[K\r\n  • \u001b[1mOption B — Stock Lots / Inventory Balances per Location\u001b[m: A single catalog \u001b[94mComponent\u001b[m has multiple \u001b[94mInventory Lot\u001b[m /\r\n  location rows, each with its own \u001b[94mlocation_id\u001b[m and \u001b[94mquantity\u001b[m. Moving a bag still reparents the \u001b[94mStorage Location\u001b[m, and\u001b[K\r\n  you can split a component's stock across multiple bags/boxes, at the cost of choosing which location when\u001b[K\r\n  adding/consuming stock (defaulting to the primary location when there's only one).\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\u001b[?7l\n\u001b[K  ➡️\u001b[?7h\u001b[5G\r\n⡿  \u001b[90mLoad\u001b[94ming..\u001b[90m.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[90m└ Tip: Use @ to mention files and give the model more context.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"]
