[0.099, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?7l\r\u001b[28A\u001b[K  ❓ \u001b[1mQ6\u001b[m - \u001b[1mDomain Term & Lot Metadata (\"Stock Lot\" vs \"Stash\")\u001b[m: When you have two separate bags of the same \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[?7h\u001b[117G\u001b[?7l\u001b[m\r\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mRecommended\u001b[m: Name this concept \u001b[1mStock Lot\u001b[m in \u001b[94mGLOSSARY.md\u001b[m (with \u001b[94mcomponent_id\u001b[m, \u001b[94mlocation_id\u001b[m, \u001b[94mquantity\u001b[m, optional\u001b[?7h\u001b[113G\r\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n⣯ \n\n\u001b[?2026l"]
[0.017, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?7l\r\u001b[29A\u001b[K  ❓ \u001b[1mQ6\u001b[m - \u001b[1mDomain Term & Lot Metadata (\"Stock Lot\" vs \"Stash\")\u001b[m: When you have two separate bags of the same \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[?7h\u001b[117G\u001b[?7l\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[K  in different \u001b[1mStorage Locations\u001b[m, what should we call each physical pile in our domain language, and what per-pile\u001b[?7h\u001b[115G\r\n  metadata (beyond \u001b[94mcomponent_id\u001b[m, \u001b[94mlocation_id\u001b[m, and \u001b[94mquantity\u001b[m) is useful to track on it?\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  For example, when you toss a new Mouser/Tayda bag into a crate, the bag itself often has a supplier part number,\r\n  order date/note, or even measured batch notes (like a bag of transistors from a specific vendor):\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  • \u001b[94mcomponent_id\u001b[m (the catalog \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m)\n\u001b[36D\u001b[94mlocation_id\u001b[m (the \u001b[1mStorage Location\u001b[m in the hierarchy, e.g., \u001b[94mCrate 1 > Big Bag B > Small Bag 4\u001b[m)\n\u001b[92D\u001b[94mquantity\u001b[m (integer count in this specific bag/location)\u001b[K\n\u001b[54D\u001b[94msupplier\u001b[m & \u001b[94msupplier_part_no\u001b[m (optional, since one bag might have come from Tayda and another from"]
[0.000, "o", " Mouser)\r\n\u001b[4C\u001b[94mlot_notes\u001b[m (optional freeform note, e.g., \u001b[94m\"Tayda 2025 order\"\u001b[m or \u001b[94m\"loose strip\"\u001b[m)\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  And for ergonomics: if a \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m only has \u001b[1mone\u001b[m Stock Lot (the 80% case once organized), the UI can let you \u001b[94m+1 / -1\u001b[m\r\n  its quantity directly inline without an extra modal; if it has \u001b[1mmultiple\u001b[m Stock Lots, the UI shows the breakdown by\u001b[K\r\n  location path (e.g., \u001b[94mTotal: 125 (85 in Crate 1 > Bag A, 40 in Box 2)\u001b[m) and lets you pick which lot to decrement (or\r\n  merge two lots when you physically combine the bags!).\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\u001b[?7l\n\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mRecommended\u001b[m: Name this concept \u001b[1mStock Lot\u001b[m in \u001b[94mGLOSSARY.md\u001b[m (with \u001b[94mcomponent_id\u001b[m, \u001b[94mlocation_id\u001b[m, \u001b[94mquantity\u001b[m, optional\u001b[?7h\u001b[113G\u001b[?7l\r\n\u001b[K  \u001b[94msupplier\u001b[m/\u001b[94msupplier_part_no\u001b[m, and \u001b[94mlot_notes\u001b[m, plus a one-click \u001b[1m\"Merge Lots\"\u001b[m action when you physically combine\u001b[?7h\u001b[109G\r\n  duplicate bags into one location). Does \u001b[1mStock Lot\u001b[m and this field/UX model feel right?\u001b[K\r"]
[0.000, "o", "\n\u001b[K\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m> sgtm\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[33;1mExplored\u001b[m 1 file \u001b[37m(GLOSSARY-FORMAT.md)\u001b[m \u001b[37m(ctrl+o to expand)\u001b[m\r\n⣯  \u001b[90mReading file...\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[90m└ Tip: Use /cs to search in your codebase.\u001b[m\n\n\u001b[40D\u001b[?2026l"]
[0.016, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\r\u001b[29A  ──────\u001b[K\r\n  ❓ \u001b[1mQ6\u001b[m - \u001b[1mDomain Term & Lot Metadata (\"Stock Lot\" vs \"Stash\")\u001b[m: When you have two separate bags of the same \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[?5W\u001b[J  in different \u001b[1mStorage Locations\u001b[m, what should we call each physical pile in our domain language, and what per-pile\r\n  metadata (beyond \u001b[94mcomponent_id\u001b[m, \u001b[94mlocation_id\u001b[m, and \u001b[94mquantity\u001b[m) is useful to track on it?\r\n\n  For example, when you toss a new Mouser/Tayda bag into a crate, the bag itself often has a supplier part number,\r\n  order date/note, or even measured batch notes (like a bag of transistors from a specific vendor):\r\n\n  • \u001b[94mcomponent_id\u001b[m (the catalog \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m)\r\n  • \u001b[94mlocation_id\u001b[m (the \u001b[1mStorage Location\u001b[m in the hierarchy, e.g., \u001b[94mCrate 1 > Big Bag B > Small Bag 4\u001b[m)\r\n  • \u001b[94mquantity\u001b[m (integer count in this specific bag/location)\r\n  • \u001b[94msupplier\u001b[m & \u001b[94msupplier_part_no\u001b[m (optional, since one bag might have come from Tayda and another from Mouser)\r\n "]
[0.000, "o", " • \u001b[94mlot_notes\u001b[m (optional freeform note, e.g., \u001b[94m\"Tayda 2025 order\"\u001b[m or \u001b[94m\"loose strip\"\u001b[m)\r\n\n  And for ergonomics: if a \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m only has \u001b[1mone\u001b[m Stock Lot (the 80% case once organized), the UI can let you \u001b[94m+1 / -1\u001b[m\r\n  its quantity directly inline without an extra modal; if it has \u001b[1mmultiple\u001b[m Stock Lots, the UI shows the breakdown by\r\n  location path (e.g., \u001b[94mTotal: 125 (85 in Crate 1 > Bag A, 40 in Box 2)\u001b[m) and lets you pick which lot to decrement (or\r\n  merge two lots when you physically combine the bags!).\u001b[?7l\r\n\n\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mRecommended\u001b[m: Name this concept \u001b[1mStock Lot\u001b[m in \u001b[94mGLOSSARY.md\u001b[m (with \u001b[94mcomponent_id\u001b[m, \u001b[94mlocation_id\u001b[m, \u001b[94mquantity\u001b[m, optional\u001b[?7h\u001b[113G\r\n  \u001b[94msupplier\u001b[m/\u001b[94msupplier_part_no\u001b[m, and \u001b[94mlot_notes\u001b[m, plus a one-click \u001b[1m\"Merge Lots\"\u001b[m action when you physically combine\r\n  duplicate bags into one location). Does \u001b[1mStock Lot\u001b[m and this field/UX model feel right?\r\n\n\u001b[90m─────────────"]
