[0.018, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\r\u001b[28A\u001b[K\r\n  • You decide to substitute a different part for the whole line (e.g., using a \u001b[94m2N5457\u001b[m instead of \u001b[94mJ201\u001b[m, or a \u001b[94m100V\u001b[m cap\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[?5W\u001b[J  instead of \u001b[94m50V\u001b[m), OR\r\n  • You only have \u001b[94m2x\u001b[m of the primary \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m in stock and want to fulfill the remaining \u001b[94m2x\u001b[m from another compatible\r\n  \u001b[1mComponent\u001b[m (or leave \u001b[94m2x\u001b[m missing for the shopping list).\r\n\n  How should substitutions and split/partial fulfillment on a \u001b[1mBOM Line\u001b[m be modeled?\r\n\n  1. \u001b[1m(Recommended) Substitutions Preserved on the BOM Line + Line-Splitting for Mixed Parts\u001b[m:\n\u001b[86D• \u001b[1mFull Line Substitution\u001b[m: Linking a \u001b[1mBOM Line\u001b[m to \u001b[3many\u001b[m catalog \u001b[94mcomponent_id\u001b[m (even if its value/part code differs\r\n\u001b[6Cfrom the original \u001b[94mraw_spec_text\u001b[m) never overwrites the original \u001b[94mraw_spec_text\u001b[m on the \u001b[1mBOM Line\u001b[m, and an optional\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[94msubstitution_note\u001b[m flag/text records why you subbed it (so the schematic spec and what you actually installed\r\n\u001b[6"]
[0.000, "o", "Cstay side by side).\n\u001b[19D• \u001b[1mPartial Allocation & Split Line Action\u001b[m: A single \u001b[1mBOM Line\u001b[m tracks \u001b[94mquantity_allocated\u001b[m (\u001b[94m0 <= quantity_allocated\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[94m<= quantity_required\u001b[m) so you can reserve/pick what you have now (e.g., \u001b[94m2 of 4\u001b[m) and the remaining shortfall (\u001b[94m2\u001b[m)\r\n\u001b[6Cautomatically flows to the shopping list. If you want to fulfill \u001b[94mC1, C2\u001b[m with Component A and \u001b[94mC3, C4\u001b[m with\r\n\u001b[6CComponent B, a 1-click \u001b[1m\"Split BOM Line\"\u001b[m action splits the row by quantity or reference designators into two \u001b[1mBOM\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[1mLines\u001b[m rather than complicating every single BOM Line with a many-to-many allocation sub-table.\r\n  2. \u001b[1mMany-to-Many \u001b[94;22mbom_line_allocations\u001b[39;1m Sub-Table on Every Line\u001b[m: Every \u001b[1mBOM Line\u001b[m can hold multiple child allocation\r\n  rows pointing to different \u001b[94mcomponent_id\u001b[ms and \u001b[94mstock_lot_id\u001b[ms simultaneously without splitting the line.\r\n  3. \u001b[1mAll-or-Nothing per BOM Line\u001b[m: A \u001b[1mBOM Line\u001b[m can only be allocated when"]
[0.000, "o", " 100% of its \u001b[94mquantity_required\u001b[m is available\r\n  from a single \u001b[1mStock Lot\u001b[m.\u001b[?7l\r\n\n\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mOption 1 (Preserve original \u001b[94;22mraw_spec_text\u001b[39;1m with \u001b[94;22msubstitution_note\u001b[39;1m + support partial \u001b[94;22mquantity_allocated\u001b[?7h\u001b[107G\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[1m(\u001b[94;22m0..quantity_required\u001b[39;1m) + 1-click \"Split BOM Line\" for mixed-component fulfillment)\u001b[m—this keeps the data model and UI\r\n  table flat and fast while handling real-world substitutions and partial stock cleanly. Does this work for you?\r\n\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m> sgtm\u001b[m\r\n⣽  \u001b[94mWor\u001b[90mking...\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[90m──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"]
