[0.016, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?7l\r\u001b[29A\u001b[K  ❓ \u001b[1mQ1\u001b[m - \u001b[1mHTTP Server, HTML Templating & Styling Stack\u001b[m: For serving server-rendered HTML and fine-grained HTMX\u001b[?7h\u001b[111G\u001b[?7l\r\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mUse Option 1 (\u001b[94;22maxum\u001b[39;1m + \u001b[94;22mtokio\u001b[39;1m + \u001b[94;22mtower-http\u001b[39;1m, \u001b[94;22mmaud\u001b[39;1m compile-time HTML functions for seamless HTMX fragment reuse, and\u001b[?7h\u001b[117G\u001b[?7l\u001b[m\r\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u001b[K  ❓ \u001b[1mQ2\u001b[m - \u001b[1mLocal Turso / libSQL Driver, Migrations & Query Strategy\u001b[m: To persist the catalog, self-referential \u001b[1mStorage\u001b[?7h\u001b[117G\u001b[m\n\u001b[13Ding dynamic\n\u001b[52D\u001b[90ma\u001b[94msset \u001b[m\n\n\n\u001b[66D\u001b[?2026l"]
[0.018, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\r\u001b[29A  ❓ \u001b[1mQ1\u001b[m - \u001b[1mHTTP Server, HTML Templating & Styling Stack\u001b[m: For serving server-rendered HTML and fine-grained HTMX\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[?5W\u001b[J  partials (like inline \u001b[1mStock Lot\u001b[m quantity adjustments, \u001b[1mBOM Line\u001b[m status toggles, and CSV reconciliation rows), we\r\n  need to pick our HTTP router, HTML templating approach, and CSS strategy:\r\n\n  1. \u001b[1mHTTP + HTML Macros (\u001b[94;22maxum\u001b[39;1m + \u001b[94;22mmaud\u001b[39;1m + zero-Node static CSS)\u001b[m:\n\u001b[55D• \u001b[94maxum\u001b[m (\u001b[94mtokio\u001b[m + \u001b[94mtower-http\u001b[m) for routing, multipart uploads, and static assets.\n\u001b[78D• \u001b[94mmaud\u001b[m for compile-time HTML macros in pure Rust—every page layout and HTMX partial (e.g., a single \u001b[94mBOM Line\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[94m<tr>\u001b[m or \u001b[94mAvailable Stock\u001b[m badge) is just a Rust function returning \u001b[94mMarkup\u001b[m, making it effortless to return either\r\n\u001b[6Ca full page or a targeted HTMX fragment from the same handler without keeping separate \u001b[94m.html\u001b[m files in sync.\r\n\u001b[6C• A self-contained static CSS stylesheet (dense tabula"]
[0.000, "o", "r workbench layout with CSS variables) and vendored \u001b[94mhtmx.\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[94mmin.js\u001b[m embedded/served directly by the Rust binary, requiring \u001b[1mzero Node.js/npm build toolchain\u001b[m.\r\n  2. \u001b[1mHTTP + Jinja-style Templates (\u001b[94;22maxum\u001b[39;1m + \u001b[94;22maskama\u001b[39;1m or \u001b[94;22mminijinja\u001b[39;1m)\u001b[m:\n\u001b[57D• Separate \u001b[94m.html\u001b[m template files using Jinja syntax, compiled into Rust structs (\u001b[94maskama\u001b[m) or loaded at runtime\r\n\u001b[6C(\u001b[94mminijinja\u001b[m).\r\n  3. \u001b[1mOther preference\u001b[m (e.g., \u001b[94mactix-web\u001b[m, Tailwind CLI build step, etc.).\u001b[?7l\r\n\n\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mUse Option 1 (\u001b[94;22maxum\u001b[39;1m + \u001b[94;22mtokio\u001b[39;1m + \u001b[94;22mtower-http\u001b[39;1m, \u001b[94;22mmaud\u001b[39;1m compile-time HTML functions for seamless HTMX fragment reuse, and\u001b[?7h\u001b[117G\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[1mvendored \u001b[94;22mhtmx.min.js\u001b[39;1m + standalone static CSS with no Node/npm toolchain)\u001b[m — does that work for you?\r\n\n\u001b[90m─────────────────────────────────────────────"]
