[0.066, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?25l\u001b[0 q\u001b[29A\u001b[94mmigrations/*.sql\u001b[39;1m auto-applied on startup, and direct parameterized SQL with a lightweight filter builder for\u001b[m\u001b[K\n\u001b[96D\u001b[1m and recursive CTEs)\u001b[m — sound good?\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m> https://crates.io/crates/turso\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n\u001b[37m○\u001b[m \u001b[37mThought for 41.8s\u001b[m \u001b[37m(Initial investigation suggests an ADR update is needed to reflect the `turso` crate dependency...)\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[33;1mExplored\u001b[m 2 searches \u001b[37m(+1 · \"turso\" crate rust sqlite compatibility json_extract OR recursive CTE OR foreign keys)\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[33m●\u001b[m \u001b[33;1mEdited\u001b[m 1 file \u001b[37m(0001-rust-htmx-turso-stack.md)\u001b[m \u001b[37m(ctrl+o to expand)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n\u001b[33;1mPending edit\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[90m──────────────────────────────────────────────"]
[0.000, "o", "──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[K\n\u001b[37;1m/Users/brett/Demos/audio-inventory-experiment/audio_inventory_skilled/docs/adr/0001-rust-htmx-turso-stack.md\u001b[m  \u001b[32m+1\u001b[m \u001b[31m-1\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[37m  \u001b[90m1 \u001b[39;48;2;53;13;14m \u001b[31m-  \u001b[39mWe are building the audio electronics inventory and DIY kit build tracker as a single-user, local-first web\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[37m    \u001b[48;2;53;13;14m \u001b[31m   \u001b[39mapplication using a Rust backend serving server-rendered HTML augmented with HTMX, persisted in a local Turso\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[37m    \u001b[48;2;53;13;14m \u001b[31m   \u001b[39m(libSQL) database. This stack keeps runtime overhead minimal, avoids client-side SPA state synchronization\u001b[K\u001b[?7l\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[K\u001b[37m    \u001b[48;2;53;13;14m \u001b[31m   \u001b[39mcomplexity for tabular/form-heavy inventory workflows, and provides a single self-contained binary with         \u001b[?7h\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[37m    \u001b[4"]
[0.000, "o", "8;2;53;13;14m \u001b[31m   \u001b[39membedded/local SQL storage.\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[37m  \u001b[90m1 \u001b[39;48;2;14;52;15m \u001b[32m+  \u001b[39mWe are building the audio electronics inventory and DIY kit build tracker as a single-user, local-first web\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[37m    \u001b[48;2;14;52;15m \u001b[32m   \u001b[39mapplication using a Rust backend (\u001b[38;2;151;198;123m`axum`\u001b[39m + \u001b[38;2;151;198;123m`maud`\u001b[39m) serving server-rendered HTML augmented with HTMX, persisted\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[37m    \u001b[48;2;14;52;15m \u001b[32m   \u001b[39min a local Turso database via the native Rust \u001b[38;2;151;198;123m`turso`\u001b[39m crate (\u001b[38;2;151;198;123m`turso::Builder::new_local`\u001b[39m). This stack keeps\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[37m    \u001b[48;2;14;52;15m \u001b[32m   \u001b[39mruntime overhead minimal, avoids client-side SPA state synchronization complexity for tabular/form-heavy\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[37m    \u001b[48;2;14;52;15m \u001b[32m   \u001b[39minventory workflows, and provides a single self-contained binary with embedded local SQL storage.\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[K\n  \u001b[94;1mshift+tab\u001b[90;22m to auto-approve file edits\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[1mAccept this file edit?\u001b[m\u001b[K\r"]
[0.000, "o", "\n\u001b[94m> 1. Yes, accept this change\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\n  2. No, reject this change\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  \u001b[94;1m↑/↓\u001b[90;22m Navigate · \u001b[94;1mtab\u001b[90;22m Amend · \u001b[94;1mf\u001b[90;22m full diff\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[?2026l"]
[19.685, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\r\u001b[30A  \u001b[1mLocation\u001b[m tree, \u001b[1mStock Lot\u001b[m balances, and \u001b[1mKit Build\u001b[m allocations in a local Turso database, while supporting dynamic\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[?5W\u001b[0 q\u001b[J  \u001b[94mjson_extract(specs_json, ...)\u001b[m parametric queries and atomic multi-statement transactions (such as deducting a \u001b[94mStock\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94mLot\u001b[m when marking a \u001b[94mBOM Line\u001b[m as \u001b[94mPicked\u001b[m):\r\n\n  1. \u001b[1mOfficial \u001b[94;22mlibsql\u001b[39;1m Crate + Embedded Startup SQL Migrations + Parameterized SQL\u001b[m:\n\u001b[75D• \u001b[1mDriver\u001b[m: Official \u001b[94mlibsql\u001b[m crate initialized via \u001b[94mlibsql::Builder::new_local(&db_path)\u001b[m (which supports local file\r\n\u001b[6Cpersistence out of the box and can optionally sync to remote Turso later if ever desired), configured on\r\n\u001b[6Cstartup with \u001b[94mPRAGMA foreign_keys = ON\u001b[m and \u001b[94mPRAGMA journal_mode = WAL\u001b[m.\n\u001b[68D• \u001b[1mMigrations\u001b[m: Numbered \u001b[94m.sql\u001b[m files in \u001b[94mmigrations/\u001b[m embedded into the binary via \u001b[94minclude_str!\u001b[m and applied\r\n\u001b[6Cautomatically on startup in a transaction (tracked in a \u001b[94m_"]
[0.000, "o", "schema_migrations\u001b[m table).\n\u001b[82D• \u001b[1mQueries\u001b[m: Parameterized SQL inside our repository implementation (with a small dynamic \u001b[94mWHERE\u001b[m-clause builder\r\n\u001b[6Cfor parametric \u001b[94mspecs_json\u001b[m filters and multi-build \u001b[1mShopping List\u001b[m aggregation), avoiding heavy ORM macro overhead\r\n\u001b[6Cthat struggles with SQLite recursive CTEs and \u001b[94mjson_extract\u001b[m.\r\n  2. \u001b[94msqlx\u001b[39;1m (SQLite driver) or an ORM (\u001b[94;22msea-orm\u001b[39;1m / \u001b[94;22mdiesel\u001b[39;1m)\u001b[m:\n\u001b[49D• Uses standard SQLite driver rather than native \u001b[94mlibsql\u001b[m, or adds ORM entity layers.\u001b[?7l\r\n\n\n\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mUse Option 1 (the official \u001b[94;22mlibsql\u001b[39;1m crate in local-file mode with \u001b[94;22mWAL\u001b[39;1m + \u001b[94;22mforeign_keys = ON\u001b[39;1m, embedded\u001b[?7h\u001b[103G\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94mmigrations/*.sql\u001b[39;1m auto-applied on startup, and direct parameterized SQL with a lightweight filter builder for\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94mjson_extract\u001b[39;1m and recursive CTEs)\u001b[m — sound good?\r\n\n\u001b[90m─────────────────────────"]
